  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEPA-Inclusive WG &amp; Climate Resilience</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Culture and Environment Preservation Association (CEPA) is a Cambodian non-governmental organization (NGO) officially registered with the Ministry of Interior on April 10, 1996. CEPA is dedicated to enhancing good governance, fostering sustainable livelihoods, enriching the natural environment, and empowering communities with the skills and technologies necessary for sustainable development. Its mission is to promote economic empowerment, rights-based approaches, advocacy, and sustainable livelihood practices. CEPA envisions an independent society that values cultural preservation and environmental protection, working in collaboration with partners, government agencies, researchers, and international communities to meet present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. CEPA is expected to implement in Stung Treng and Kratie provinces to deliver key interventions encompassto achieve IP3 expected outcome of three interconnected pillars: Gender Equality, Disability, and Social Inclusion (GEDSI) and Feminist Influencing, Water Resource Governance (WRG), and Climate Resilient Communities. Pillar 1 aims to advance the leadership, agency, and representation of diverse women leaders, including Indigenous women, People withDisabilities, and marginalized social groups,to influence WRG and climate resilience policies and practices. It serves as a flagship initiative that integrates with the other two pillars. Pillar 2 focuses on empowering civil society networks and coalitions to actively engage in WRG fora and policy processes, ensuring that diverse perspectives&#x2014;particularly those of youth, People with Disabilities, and Indigenous Peoples&#x2014;are included. Pillar 3 promotes resilience in riverine communities in the lower Mekongbasin by addressing human and climate-driven changes through integrated community management approaches, such as climate-resilient livelihoods, disaster risk management, and sustainable freshwater and fisheries management. These interventions collectivelyaddress critical transboundary water resource governance issues and climate change impacts, including river diversions, hydropower infrastructure, changing flow regimes, water pollution, and habitat loss,with a strong focus on inclusivity, sustainability, and community empowerment for effective water governance and climate resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Culture and Environment Preservatio</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Rivers Coalition in Cambodia (RCC) has been progressing since its early formation in 2007 to support farmers and fishers groups, in particular the dam-affected communities, to influence government policies related to water governance of the Mekong byupholdingcommunities&#x92; rights to be consulted on various development initiatives affecting their food security and livelihoods. RCC is a loose network of 28 organizations, consisting of both national and international NGOs, and 12 regional partners. In order to become moreeffective in representing their constituency, the Action will accommodate RCC to define its future strategies and plans by seeking strategic complementarities (clarities on roles and responsibilities) among all RCC&#x92;s members. The network is led by its members, while the NGO Forum on Cambodia, a broad advocacy network of more than 80 NGOs in Cambodia, is acting as its secretariat. Funding for the main activities of the RCC is therefore already funded through the NGO-Forum. In view of this project CEPA was selected to be the main actor in this proposal as a key member of the RCC. This is in the understanding that support to an active member of the RCC and with close focus on support to networking activities  also the RCC would be strengthened. Additionally CEPA (like other active members as 3SPN, and FACT) is working closely with a range of groups of farmers and fishers, who are target groups for the Action, along the Mekong, Sesan, Srepok and Sekong Rivers as well as the Tonle Sap area.The Action will provide assistance to all members of RCC via CEPA and at the same time strengthen the network to become more effective in organising the fishers and farmers groups.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
